"],"label":"Urine Ammonium Oxalate Crystal Measurement","obo_id":"NCIT:C74759","type":"class","exact_synonyms":["Urine Ammonium Oxalate Crystal Measurement"]},{"iri":"http://id.nlm.nih.gov/mesh/D019815","ontology_name":"mesh","related_synonyms":["Acid, Oxalic","Aluminum Oxalate","Ammonium Oxalate","Chromium (2+) Oxalate","Chromium (3+) Oxalate (3:2)","Chromium Oxalate","Diammonium Oxalate","Dilithium Oxalate","Dipotassium Oxalate","Disodium Oxalate","Ferric Oxalate","Iron (2+) Oxalate (1:1)","Iron (3+) Oxalate","Iron Oxalate","Magnesium Oxalate (1:1)","Magnesium Oxalate","Manganese (2+) Oxalate (1:1)","Monoammonium Oxalate","Monohydrogen Monopotassium Oxalate","Monopotassium Oxalate","Monosodium Oxalate","Oxalate, Aluminum","Oxalate, Chromium","Oxalate, Diammonium","Oxalate, Dilithium","Oxalate, Dipotassium","Oxalate, Disodium","Oxalate, Ferric","Oxalate, Iron","Oxalate, Magnesium","Oxalate, Monoammonium","Oxalate, Monohydrogen Monopotassium","Oxalate, Monopotassium","Oxalate, Monosodium","Oxalate, Potassium Chromium","Oxalate, Potassium","Oxalate, Sodium","Potassium Chromium Oxalate","Potassium Oxalate (2:1)","Potassium Oxalate","Sodium Oxalate"],"ontology_prefix":"mesh","short_form":"mesh_D019815","description":["A strong dicarboxylic acid occurring in many plants and vegetables. It is produced in the body by metabolism of glyoxylic acid or ascorbic acid. It is not metabolized but excreted in the urine. It is used as an analytical reagent and general reducing agent."],"label":"Oxalic Acid","obo_id":"mesh:D019815","type":"class"},{"iri":"http://id.nlm.nih.gov/mesh/C517490","ontology_name":"mesh","related_synonyms":["AHOH cpd"],"ontology_prefix":"mesh","short_form":"mesh_C517490","description":[],"label":"ammonium hydrogen oxalate hemihydrate","obo_id":"mesh:C517490","type":"class"},{"iri":"http://id.nlm.nih.gov/mesh/C009410","ontology_name":"mesh","related_synonyms":["C14QAC","Catrimox-14","TDTMA cpd","TTAB cpd","myristyl trimethylammonium bromide","tetradecyl-trimethylammonium bromide","tetradecyltrimethylammonium bromide","tetradecyltrimethylammonium chloride","tetradecyltrimethylammonium hydroxide","tetradecyltrimethylammonium iodide","tetradecyltrimethylammonium mesylate","tetradecyltrimethylammonium oxalate","trimethyl tetradecyl ammonium bromide","trimethyltetradecylammonium bromide"],"ontology_prefix":"mesh","short_form":"mesh_C009410","description":[],"label":"tetradecyltrimethylammonium","obo_id":"mesh:C009410","type":"class"},{"iri":"http://id.nlm.nih.gov/mesh/D014545","ontology_name":"mesh","related_synonyms":["Calculi, Urinary","Calculus, Urinary","Stone, Urinary Tract","Stone, Urinary","Stones, Urinary Tract","Stones, Urinary","Urinary Calculus","Urinary Stone","Urinary Stones","Urinary Tract Stone","Urinary Tract Stones"],"ontology_prefix":"mesh","short_form":"mesh_D014545","description":["Low-density crystals or stones in any part of the URINARY TRACT. Their chemical compositions often include CALCIUM OXALATE, magnesium ammonium phosphate (struvite), CYSTINE, or URIC ACID."],"label":"Urinary Calculi","obo_id":"mesh:D014545","type":"class"},{"iri":"http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NCIT_C47388","ontology_name":"ncit","ontology_prefix":"NCIT","short_form":"NCIT_C47388","description":["The chloride salt form of ambenonium, a quaternary ammonium compound with parasympathomimetic activity. Ambenonium chloride is a rapid indirect-acting cholinergic agonist that reversibly blocks the activity of acetylcholinesterase, thereby prevents acetylcholine hydrolysis and prolonging its activity on nicotinic receptors at the neuromuscular junction."],"label":"Ambenonium Chloride","obo_id":"NCIT:C47388","type":"class","exact_synonyms":["(Oxalylbis(iminoethylene))bis((o-chlorobenzyl)diethyl-ammonium), Dichloride","AMBENONIUM CHLORIDE","Ambenonium Chloride","Misuran","Mytelase","N,N\u0027-((1,2-Dioxo-1,2-ethanediyl)bis(imino-2,1-ethanediyl))bis(2-chloro-N,N-diethyl-benzenemethanaminium), Dichloride","Oxazyl","Win 8077"]},{"iri":"http